WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.840
Astronomers detected the first radio signal directly from an exoplanet 63 light years away. This is Fascinating Frontiers, Episode 215. Astronomers recorded repeating radio bursts from Beta-Pictora's B using the MIRCAT array in South Africa. The planet is roughly 12 times more massive than Jupiter and orbits a star about 23 million years old. The bursts are attributed to auroras generated by the planet's magnetic field.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:33.560
The field strength is estimated at least 200 times stronger than Earth's.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.380
Observations distinguish the planetary signal from emission by the host star.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.700
From detecting activity around a living star system, we turn to what happens after a

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:44.300
star dies.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:50.440
Dr. Alcandidate Jamie Williams at the University of Warwick identified niobium signatures

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:57.340
in archival Hubble observations of white dwarf HS0209 plus 0832.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:04.400
2. The element was first recorded in 1999 among roughly 100 unidentified chemical features.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:11.020
Far ultraviolet spectroscopic explorer data confirmed the niobium abundance. The material

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.480
formed after the star became a white dwarf rather than during its main sequence phase.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:21.400
Williams noted the white dwarf stage may represent only the first chapter of a longer planetary

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:29.720
theory story. The Hubble detection of niobium around white dwarf HS0209 plus 0832 points

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:35.320
to material synthesized after the star's death. Niobium does not form through standard

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:41.260
stellar fusion, yet appears in high abundance in the archival spectra. Williams and colleagues

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:46.040
trace the element to processes that occurred once the star had become a compact remnant.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:51.620
The findings suggest some planetary systems continue assembling long after their hostars

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:52.900
leave the main sequence.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.320
Follow-up ultraviolet observations could map additional heavy elements and test whether

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.660
the material is actively accruing onto a forming world.

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:07.580
A typical white dwarf packs the mass of the sun into a sphere the size of Earth, creating

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:11.440
surface gravity 200,000 times stronger than what we feel here.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.960
Any planet that forms afterward must draw from gas and dust released during the star's

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:22.120
final red giant phase, or from debris scattered by earlier planetary migration.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:30.480
In the case of HS0209 plus 0832, the niobium signature implies the material condensed

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:35.560
and accreted after the star had already shed its outer layers, a process that can take

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:36.980
hundreds of millions of years.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:41.580
measures. The same gravitational settling that pulls heavy elements downward in the white

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:47.280
dwarf's atmosphere also concentrates them in observable layers, turning the star into

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:53.660
a natural spectrometer. Yet no model fully explains how enough mass survives to build a detectable

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:57.480
planet rather than simply falling inward or being ejected.

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:04.080
The open question is whether such second-generation worlds are rare curiosities, or a common endpoint

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.920
for systems whose first planets were destroyed during the Star's expansion.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:14.320
While one system is still building worlds, another astronaut is wrapping up her own journey in orbit.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:18.760
ESA astronaut Sophie Adinot and her crew-12 crewmates

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:25.000
undocked from the International Space Station at around 1205 Greenwich Mean Time on 7 October

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:34.060
2026. The Dragon spacecraft performed burns to move away from the zenith facing port of the

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:38.920
protects the capsule from plasma reaching 1,600 degrees Celsius during reentry.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.700
Splashed down off the coast of California is scheduled for 8 October at 1534

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.960
Greenwich Mean Time. Sophie will then fly to Cologne for post-flight activities at the

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:56.520
European Astronaut Center. She completed three spacewalks totaling 19 hours and 42 minutes during the

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:01.580
mission. Human missions keep the station running. Robotic ones are heading for the moon.

00:04:01.580 --> 00:04:08.620
The new RUVER model was unveiled at the QUT Yandewaniba Lunar Test Facility during World Space Week.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.280
QUT researchers are developing positioning systems for semi-autonomous navigation on the lunar surface.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:21.380
The rover is scheduled to reach the moon in 2030 as Australia's first spacecraft to do so.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:25.760
It will carry a NASA payload for surface analysis and exploration.

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:30.340
20 Australian organizations form the RUVER consortium.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:35.660
While one nation prepares hardware for the moon, researchers elsewhere are tracking what

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:36.780
is already up there.

00:04:37.540 --> 00:04:44.640
Computer scientist Alan Snibben operates the 12-foot-wide SOHBRIT facility at Sandia and

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:45.760
National Laboratories.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:51.660
The system uses commercial off-the-shelf components and runs autonomously through scheduled night

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:52.400
observations.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:55.900
On a clear night, it can track about 80 satellites.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:00.600
data support collision prediction and orbit monitoring for the space mission

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.320
program. Schnibben developed scheduling software that allows remote operation

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:09.940
from his desk. Monitoring objects in Earth orbit leads us to the larger

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:15.560
question of how galaxies themselves evolve. Researchers use the Astrid simulation

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:21.640
to follow black holes across 12.6 billion years of cosmic evolution. The model

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:26.700
Several tracks galaxies with stellar masses from 10 million to one trillion solar masses.

00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:31.920
Wandering black holes were found to be more common in lower mass galaxies where central

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:32.960
gravity is weaker.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.520
The study was led by Yale-PhD candidate Emma Jane Weller.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.760
Results appear in the astrophysical journal letters.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:46.380
Black holes also influence their surroundings through jets as new observations show.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:53.520
Observations combine desi-survey spectra with low-far radiojet data from hundreds of active galaxies.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.580
Ionized hydrogen emission aligns strongly with the axes of radio jets.

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:05.540
Jets heat gas in the circumgalactic medium out to distances 10-20 times the visible galaxy radius.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.720
The process can both quench and stimulate star formation depending on location.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:17.940
Sanchatita Borthakur of Arizona State University and Namrata Roy of the Raman Research Institute

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:23.820
led the work. From cosmic scale processes, we return to our own planet's changing climate.

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:31.400
CIS extend hit its lowest point on 12 September 2026 at 4.6 million square kilometers.

00:06:31.980 --> 00:06:39.900
This value ties 2008, 2010, and 2025 for the 10th lowest minimum in the satellite record.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:48.100
Every summer from 2007 through 2026, ranks among the 20 lowest extents observed since 1978.

00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:52.820
The 2026 minimum occurred earlier than the long-term average.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:56.900
NASA and the National Snow and Ice Data Center produced the measurements.

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:00.820
Back on Mars, Curiosity continues its long climb.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:09.160
The Panorama was captured on the 4,980 second Martian Day of the Mission, at 8.30am local time.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:13.280
Yardang's appear as jagged wind-carved ridges in the distance.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:17.180
Bright morning light creates blue hues in the foreground terrain.

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:21.600
The image highlights features scientists have awaited for close examination.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:27.160
While one rover works on Mars, another cargo craft prepares to leave the station.

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:33.120
The spacecraft delivered more than 11,000 pounds of supplies and experiments earlier this year.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:40.380
Canadarm 2 will release it from the Unity Module's Earth Facing Port at 12.45pm Eastern Daylight

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:40.680
Time.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:45.680
Cygnus XL will de-orbit on 11 October for a destructive re-entry.

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:51.760
NASA will stream live coverage of the undocking beginning at 12.30pm Eastern Daylight Time.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:54.960
The mission launched on a Falcon 9 in April.

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.060
With station operations continuing, we close with a quick look at other mission updates.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:05.960
Next time, we will be watching for the European Mars Rover prototype results after its recent

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:07.160
desert field test.

